---
import InsightArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/InsightArticleLayout.astro';
export const prerender = true;
const title = 'How to Find Local Businesses That May Need a Better Website';
const description = 'Learn seven practical signals web designers can use to identify local businesses worth researching, from mobile usability and broken pages to forms and booking.';
const path = '/business-intelligence/insights/find-businesses-that-need-web-design';
const pinConcepts = ['7 Signals a Local Business May Need a Better Website','How Web Designers Can Find Local Clients','Stop Cold Prospecting Random Businesses','How to Research Local Business Prospects'];
---
<InsightArticleLayout {title} {description} {path} eyebrow="Web design prospecting" intro="A weak website can create a real business-development opportunity. The trick is separating observable friction from the lazy assumption that every dated site needs to be replaced." {pinConcepts}>
  <h2>Start with business impact, not visual taste</h2>
  <p>A website can look old and still do its job. Another can look polished while quietly losing inquiries through a broken form, an unclear call to action, or a miserable mobile experience. Prospecting improves when you look for friction tied to an actual business outcome.</p>
  <p>Your first pass should answer two questions: what can a visitor not do easily, and what evidence supports that conclusion? That keeps outreach specific and prevents the “your website looks outdated” message from joining the landfill of identical agency pitches.</p>

  <h2>Seven signals worth investigating</h2>
  <h3>1. Mobile usability creates obvious friction</h3>
  <p>Check whether text is readable, buttons are comfortably tappable, menus work, and forms fit the screen. A mobile issue matters most when the likely customer is searching from a phone and needs to call, book, request a quote, or find a location quickly.</p>
  <h3>2. Important pages are broken or missing</h3>
  <p>Test navigation, contact pages, service pages, and visible calls to action. Broken links, server errors, expired security warnings, or missing images are observable problems. Record the exact page and behavior instead of declaring the whole website broken.</p>
  <h3>3. The form asks too little—or far too much</h3>
  <p>A name-and-email form may fail to collect the information needed to route a roofing, landscaping, or commercial-service inquiry. A giant form can also destroy completion rates. Look at whether the intake matches the sales process: service type, location, urgency, photos, budget range, or appointment preference may matter.</p>
  <h3>4. There is no clear booking or conversion path</h3>
  <p>Some services need online booking; others need a qualified estimate request. The useful signal is not simply “no booking.” It is a mismatch between how the customer wants to act and what the website allows them to do.</p>
  <h3>5. Service-area coverage is thin</h3>
  <p>A local operator serving several cities may have one generic homepage and no useful location context. That can create both an information gap for visitors and a local-search opportunity. Avoid recommending dozens of near-duplicate pages; useful coverage needs distinct local relevance.</p>
  <h3>6. The conversion path is unclear</h3>
  <p>Visitors should understand the service, geographic coverage, next step, and basic reason to trust the company. If every page ends without a meaningful action—or offers three competing actions with equal weight—the website may be generating attention without direction.</p>
  <h3>7. The company has outgrown the site</h3>
  <p>Additional locations, new service lines, commercial work, active hiring, financing, or a larger crew can indicate that the business is more sophisticated than its current site suggests. These are context signals, not proof. Verify the growth before using it in outreach.</p>

  <div class="bi-callout"><strong>One signal rarely proves a redesign is needed.</strong> A cluster of verified problems tied to customer action is far more useful than a subjective design critique.</div>

  <h2>Turn observations into a qualified opportunity</h2>
  <ol><li>Confirm you found the official website.</li><li>Record the exact page and observable issue.</li><li>Check whether the issue affects a meaningful customer action.</li><li>Look for business signals that make improvement economically relevant.</li><li>Verify there is no obvious reason your service would be a poor fit.</li></ol>
  <p>A strong first sentence names a real observation without pretending you already understand the company’s internal numbers. “I noticed the estimate path does not collect project location or photos” is credible. “You are losing thousands every month” is theater wearing a necktie.</p>

  <h2>Respect the “do not pitch” result</h2>
  <p>Sometimes the website is modern, booking works, service pages are strong, and no meaningful gap is visible. That is useful intelligence. Remove the business from the redesign list or investigate a different service only when a separate signal supports it. Better qualification means fewer names and better reasons.</p>
</InsightArticleLayout>
